#N canvas 100 100 600 480 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win;
#X msg 40 100 create;
#X msg 130 100 destroy;
#X msg 250 100 start;
#X msg 340 100 stop;
#X obj 40 150 jet_head 0;
#X obj 40 180 jet_video;
#X obj 40 210 jet_shader slitscan_line.metal;
#X obj 40 240 jet_rectangle 4.2 3.2;
#X msg 250 150 maxdelay 10;
#X msg 340 150 maxdelay 40;
#X msg 250 190 reverse 1;
#X msg 340 190 reverse 0;
#X msg 250 230 freeze 1;
#X msg 340 230 freeze 0;
#X text 40 280 jet_shader slitscan_line.metal - the row-based slit-scan as a shader file. Each horizontal line comes from a different moment: the delay grows from the top to the bottom of the picture ("reverse 1" turns it around). "@history 40" in the file keeps the last 40 frames of the input in a GPU texture array \, jet_history() reads the frame N steps back. maxdelay and reverse are ordinary parameters (inlets 1 and 2 of the object \, or send them by name as here). freeze 1 stops recording so you can watch the frozen history scroll by. Edit shaders/slitscan_line.metal while it runs and save - the picture follows.;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 6 0;
#X connect 4 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 9 0 7 0;
#X connect 10 0 7 0;
#X connect 11 0 7 0;
#X connect 12 0 7 0;
#X connect 13 0 7 0;
#X connect 14 0 7 0;
